{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_237duajub72xinh5hhacwdaxyhp2xlpe45m5z5u4dgtmvnp5ayya","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Result: the Rust translation is correct — it needed **zero** fixes\n\n`src/lib.rs` is byte-identical (md5 verified) to what I started with. Every divergence I chased turned out to be a bug in *my own* test code or docs, not in the translation.\n\n**Build surface (Phase A):** `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]` section and `CMakeLists.txt` exposes no options or `#ifdef` branches — so there is exactly **one** valid feature combination (the empty set). I verified it under both `--no-default-features` and default, in **debug and release**.\n\n**Artifacts created:**\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — 8/8 symbols match exactly (7 functions + the `matrix` data symbol), same type and 48-byte size; symbol diff empty in both directions. All Rust undefined symbols resolve to libc/libgcc.\n- `ERRORS.md` — 15 rows, derived by classifying all 13 `return` statements in the C, plus generic boundary cases G1–G6.\n- `CONFIGS.md` — 25 rows across the flag/mode, global-state, and container-shape axes.\n- 45 tests in 4 binaries, all loading **both** `.so`s via `libloading` and calling only exported symbols.\n- `run_verification.sh` (feature-combo sweep) and `mutation_test.sh` (suite self-validation).\n\n**Three things worth flagging:**\n\n1. **A real blind spot in my first suite, found by mutation testing.** Changing the Rust `matrix` initializer (`0xD4`→`0xD5`) left the suite **green**. Cause: every matrix-touching test overwrote the shared global with a harness constant before comparing, so the exported data symbol's *initializer* was never differentially tested. Fixed by adding `tests/differential_initial_state.rs` — deliberately a single test in its own binary so it observes both libraries in pristine, never-written state. Mutation score is now **12 caught / 0 missed**.\n\n2. **Two silent false-passes in my own tooling**, both corrected:\n   - `run_verification.sh` reported `missing: none` while `/tmp` was read-only and the symbol diff never actually ran (now uses `$TMPDIR`).\n   - `mutation_test.sh` grepped only for uppercase `FAILED`, so a mutation that *aborted* the test binary (`free_array` NULL-guard removal → SIGABRT) was scored as missed.\n\n3. **`cargo test` alone does not build the cdylib.** Nothing links it, so the `.so` is never produced and every test would fail on a clean checkout. The harness now builds it on demand (once per process), keeping bare `cargo test` self-sufficient without touching `[lib] crate-type`.\n\nI also corrected an error in my own `ERRORS.md`: I had listed `(1<<62)+1` as an allocation-failure trigger, but the `size_t` multiply wraps to `malloc(4)`, which **succeeds** — it returns a valid handle whose `capacity` is 2^62+1 against a 4-byte buffer. That became its own row (15), and it's precisely the case where a `checked_mul` in Rust would wrongly reject; the translation correctly uses `wrapping_mul`. Notably, `expand_array`'s dangling-pointer behaviour after `realloc(p, 0)` is faithfully preserved rather than \"fixed\", which is correct per the instructions."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":967,"cache_read_input_tokens":125656,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":967,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b1d70fe-eda8-4da7-b4bc-9e432c8c0bbf","uuid":"79349756-fac0-4552-bee9-26394121d99c","timestamp":"2026-08-19T21:51:13.286Z"}
